---
const jobs = [
  ["Conjunction", "Joins a clause. A clause has a subject and a verb.", "We stayed in because it rained."],
  ["Linking adverb", "Links one sentence or event to another.", "It rained. Therefore, we stayed in."],
  ["Preposition", "Relates a following noun phrase to the sentence.", "We stayed in during the storm."]
];
---

<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Connecting Ideas - Three jobs</h1>
  <p>
    A connecting word can show <strong>time, place, reason, result, contrast</strong> or <strong>condition</strong>. First decide the meaning you need, then check the grammar of the words that follow.
  </p>
  <section class="summary-block summary-peach">
    <h2>Conjunction, adverb or preposition</h2>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Word type</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">What it does</th>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          jobs.map(([type, does, example]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{type}</th>
              <td>{does}</td>
              <td>{example}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-yellow">
    <h2>What comes next?</h2>
    <p><strong>Before lunch</strong> — <em>before</em> is followed by the noun phrase <em>lunch</em>, so it is a preposition.</p>
    <p><strong>Before we ate lunch</strong> — <em>before</em> is followed by the clause <em>we ate lunch</em>, so it is a conjunction.</p>
    <p><strong>We ate before</strong> — nothing follows <em>before</em>. Here it is an adverb.</p>
    <p>This is a useful first test. Not every connector fits it perfectly.</p>
  </section>
</div>
